body:
### Tested versions

used version 4.4.1 

### System information

Windows 11 | Nvidea GeForce RTX3050 6GB Laptop GPU

### Issue description

Im using particle2D to emulate the aiming of a "brick blaster" like game.  Using the player mouse to get the aim rotation.
Using the colision mode "rigid" in my process material. The feature i was expecting is that the particles would simulate the path that my projectile would make, and for this reason i would expect it to bounce in the tilemap.

the colisions are working as long as i dont change the aim, but when i move the aim, it seems that the particles colides with something that is not instantiated.

<img width="1897" height="935" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/382e23f9-8948-4cb6-8173-0671708f8136" />

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2f686d7e-ed21-495b-84c5-cfca2ea3bc1a

```
extends Node2D

@export var ball_scene: PackedScene
@export var shoot_force: float = 300.0
@export var aim_length: float = 250.0
@export var max_balls: int = 0
var current_balls: int = 5

@onready var spawn_point = $Canhao/SpawnPoint
@onready var aim_pivot = $Canhao/AimPivot
@onready var cannon = $Canhao
@onready var particulas = $Canhao/AimPivot/GPUParticles2D
var shoot_direction = Vector2.RIGHT


func _ready():
	current_balls = max_balls
	print("PLAYER INSTANCIADO")


func _process(_delta):
	update_aim()

	if Input.is_action_just_pressed("shoot"):
		shoot()



func update_aim():
	var mouse_pos = get_global_mouse_position()
	shoot_direction = (mouse_pos - spawn_point.global_position).normalized()
	cannon.rotation = shoot_direction.angle()
```

### Steps to reproduce



Press "play" button
after scene "gameplay" loads, wait a couple of seconds move quickly the mouse left and right above the cannon.
You will perceive that the particle behavior will be very odd due to the fact it is colliding with something that does not exists

